WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 9, Backend engineering. A model in a notebook is a science fair project. A model behind a validated endpoint, with a stable contract and a clear failure mode, is a product. This phase makes the service real. This phase has 2 lessons. Lesson 1: Serve with FastAPI. A model service validates inputs, loads one versioned artifact, performs identical preprocessing, returns a stable contract, and emits useful telemetry. The question for this lesson: How does a trained model become a dependable API?. A model in a notebook is a science fair project. An API is a product. Someone else's code will call it at an inconvenient hour and expect an answer in milliseconds, not a stack trace. FastAPI makes the contract explicit. Pydantic validates the input, the response schema stays stable, and the model loads once at startup. The important part is not the framework; it is that training and serving run the exact same preprocessing. You will learn: Pydantic creates explicit input and output contracts., Load models at startup, not per request., Health checks, timeouts, structured errors, and version logging are product behavior.. Practice: "Build health and predict endpoints for a saved pipeline with validation. Watch out for: Loading the model per request., Unvalidated JSON., Reimplementing preprocessing in API code.. You are ready when: Validate schema., Return stable fields., Log latency and version.. Recommended video: FastAPI Course for Beginners. Lesson 2: REST, auth, and async. Design resources and status codes, separate authentication from authorization, and keep blocking model work off the async event loop. The question for this lesson: Can the service survive real clients and real mistakes?. A prediction endpoint is a contract. The request schema, status codes, error shape, and versioning tell clients what to expect. Authentication proves who is calling; authorization decides what they may do. Async helps with waiting, not with CPU work. Database and network calls can await. A heavy model prediction usually belongs in a worker thread, a queue, or a separate service, or it will block every other request. You will learn: Use status codes and error bodies that a client can act on., Authentication and authorization are separate decisions., Async I/O and CPU-bound inference need different concurrency strategies.. Practice: "Add API-key auth. Watch out for: Putting secrets in source control., Running CPU-heavy inference directly on the event loop., Returning stack traces or internal model details to clients.. You are ready when: Document the contract., Test auth failures., Load test the blocking path.. A documented prediction API with validation, auth, and sane async behavior. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
